// src/v4/lens/synthesis.dag
//
// Scope: cross-algorithm complexity lens — prove a DIFFERENT program achieves
//        better complexity than user's, for the same I/O relation. Distinct
//        from lens/complexity.dag (same-algorithm tightness via semantics-
//        preserving rewrites); synthesis reasons ACROSS program shapes (e.g.,
//        bubble-sort → merge-sort, naive-matmul → Strassen).
// Anchor: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Program_synthesis +
//         docs/r4-carve-out-routing.md C7 (advisory carrier, NOT fail-closed
//         Diagnostic; algorithm choice is design-tier — programmer decides)
//
// Owns:
//   - lens read: Node -> Witness<Report>  (Report carrier, NOT Diagnostic)
//   - semantic-equivalence relation (when do two programs compute the same
//     input→output relation? structural, not behavioral)
//   - pattern-recognition substrate (match user program against algorithm-
//     class templates: sort / search / matmul / graph-traverse / etc.)
//   - transformation rule library (algorithm-class rewrites)
//   - cross-algorithm cost comparison (composes with lens/complexity.dag)
//
// Discipline (per INVARIANTS C-8 + r4-carve-out-routing.md C7):
//   - The LENS itself is advisory (emits Report, not Diagnostic) — algorithm
//     choice is design-tier; programmer informed, programmer chooses
//   - A user's apply_lens(synthesis, Enforce { ... }) declaration converts
//     advisory Report into fail-closed Diagnostic per their policy choice
//   - Default policy: Introspect (advisory) per opt-in-depth discipline
//
// Consumes:
//   - std/node.dag, std/witness.dag, std/algebra.dag
//   - std/report.dag (advisory carrier)
//   - lens/complexity.dag (current-complexity input for cross-algorithm comparison)
//
// Status: scaffold — fill per TASKS.md T-17
// Brief: see BRIEF_TEMPLATE.md. T-17 is XL scope, research-tier risk —
// substrate additions WILL surface that may trigger operator escalation
// (e.g., semantic-equivalence shape, pattern-recognition substrate
// shape). STOP-and-escalate discipline applies fully.

module v4.lens.synthesis

